<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Fabricator.FluidPlugin</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Fabricator.FluidPlugin</AssemblyName>
    <Description>
      The Fluid/Liquid template engine: a connection-free IProvider contributing the global scalar
      fabricator_render(template, params) and the SQL-generating table function fluid_query(template, params).

      ⚠ IT IS A BUILT-IN PROVIDER ASSEMBLY, NOT A PLUGIN, since 2026-09-02 — discovered by name through
      ProviderRegistry's FABRICATOR_BACKEND_ASSEMBLY list exactly like Fabricator.Delta, and published into
      the managed directory by publish-managed.ps1. It was a bundled PLUGIN for one day, and the reason it
      stopped being one is a footgun rather than tidiness: FABRICATOR_PLUGIN_DIR REPLACES the plugin roots
      rather than extending them (PluginPaths.ResolveRoots returns early), so a user who set it to load
      their OWN plugin silently lost fabricator_render. The bundled root had exactly one occupant, and it
      was the one capability documented as available out of the box.

      It remains its OWN PROJECT, which is what keeps the cost of the Fluid dependency contained: the AOT
      SKU drops one entry from the backend list (docs/aot-bridge.md risk R2 — Parlot's compiled mode needs
      System.Linq.Expressions), where a merge back into Fabricator.SqlServer would put a template engine
      inside the SQL Server backend again.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The contract, plus the reusable middle (ArrowValueReader.ReadTimestamp). Ordinary references now:
         as a published provider assembly this lands in the same managed directory as the bridge, so the
         Private="false" the PLUGIN form needed — to avoid a second, non-assignable copy of every contract
         type beside the plugin — no longer applies. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fabricator.Abstractions\Fabricator.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fabricator.Common\Fabricator.Common.csproj" />

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ A PRERELEASE PIN, deliberately and with its own review rule: we track Fluid v3.0, whose only
      published form is a beta. A prerelease is not a stable contract — the API may move between betas — so
      a bump here is a CODE-COMPATIBILITY question, not a routine version bump, and the gate that answers it
      is test/verify_plugin_fluid.test. Move to the stable 3.0.0 as soon as it ships.
      ⚠ Being a built-in raises the stakes: this now loads in every payload rather than only where a plugin
      root was configured. It already shipped in every RELEASE artifact (pack-distribution staged it as a
      bundled plugin), so the bytes are not new — what is new is that a dev build carries it too.

      /!\ AND THERE IS TIMEZONE WORK ON FLUID'S MAIN BRANCH THAT THIS BETA DOES NOT CARRY (user-reported
      2026-09-01, not verified here). The TEMPORAL assertions in verify_plugin_fluid are therefore the
      ones most likely to move at the next bump - which is what they are for: they pin today's rendering
      and ordering EXACTLY, so a change arrives as a failed assertion naming the value rather than as a
      silently different result. If they move, re-derive docs/fluid-templating.md section 7.4a rather
      than editing the expected values to match; that section also holds a DEFERRED decision (an
      ISO-string value model) whose case depends on what upstream does here.
      ⚠ It is ALSO why this stays a PackageReference rather than a source reference to a local clone: a
      sibling-path reference pins nothing and no clone but the author's can build it - exactly the shape
      this repo converted engineered-wood and DuckDB.ExtensionKit away from - and it would leave CI
      silently gating a DIFFERENT Fluid than the developer runs.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Fluid.Core" Version="3.0.0-beta.7" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
